Chapter 5
Express Terms of the Agreement: Principles of Interpretation and the Parol Evidence Rule

At this point in our studies, we have seen enough to realize that issues affecting the outcome of contractual disputes can be divided roughly into questions of “substance” and “form.” To the extent that contract law is concerned with the actual assent of the parties to an asserted contract, one might characterize this as concern with the “substance” of their agreement. Questions such as “Was an offer truly made?” or “Was an acceptance manifested?” would seem to fall into this category. By contrast, the various “statutes of frauds” discussed in the preceding chapter seem to be requirements not of substance, but only of “form”—the expression of an agreement in a particular medium and with a particular indicium of assent. Of course, this distinction between substance and form tends (like many others) to blur somewhat on closer examination: The requirement of “definiteness,” for instance, seems (for some courts at least) to be an issue of form as well as substance; thoughtful application of the statute of frauds is likely to be concerned not merely with the minimum requirements of form but also with the issue of real agreement. And commentators have debated whether the requirement of consideration is truly one of form or of substance (or both). Nevertheless, the distinction between form and substance has appeared to many to be a useful one. See, e.g., Lon Fuller, Consideration and Form, 41 Colum. L. Rev. 799 (1941); P.S. Atiyah & Robert S. Summers, Form and Substance in Anglo-American Law (1987).

Chapter 5 continues this parallel and to some extent overlapping examination of form and substance in contract law. In fact, the subjects covered in this chapter may be the areas of contract law where the contest between formalist and substantive perspectives is at its most intense. See generally Joshua M. Silverstein, The Contract Interpretation Policy Debate: A Primer, 26 Stan. J. L. Bus. & Fin. 222 (2021).

Section A is devoted to the methods employed by courts to interpret the parties’ expressions of assent. Even where the parties have initially agreed on p. 388particular words and phrases to describe their contemplated exchange of performances, they may disagree later about the meaning that should be attached to those manifestations of agreement. As we saw in the prior chapter, the law does not require all types of contracts to be in writing in order to be enforceable, and even those that are within the scope of a statute of frauds usually do not have to be written out in complete and exhaustive detail. But contracts are frequently written, and questions of interpretation virtually always concern that type of agreement.

Section B examines an issue closely related to interpretation and that only arises when the parties have chosen to memorialize their agreement in a written document. Where the parties have prepared a written agreement, one of them may later contend that the writing does not accurately or completely express their “true” agreement—that there were additional agreements made between them not contained in the writing, or even that the writing in some respect describes their agreement in an inaccurate or misleading fashion. Courts over the years have worked out a method of screening evidence of such matters through the application of the so-called “parol evidence rule.” The meaning and application of that rule, as well as its relationship to interpretation, is examined in the latter part of this chapter.

Law students, lawyers, and judges typically find interpretation and the parol evidence rule harder to comprehend than other areas of contract law. Accordingly, this chapter contains more explanatory material than other chapters in the book. The detailed introductions, notes, and comments included here will facilitate your understanding of the challenging topics that make up this chapter.

A.Principles of Interpretation

The nature of interpretation. Contract interpretation is the process of determining the meaning of the language of a contract. See Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 200. The goal of contract interpretation is to ascertain the intent of the parties. Steven J. Burton, Elements of Contract Interpretation § 1.1 (2009). But accomplishing this task can be difficult. Party intent is often unclear and disputed. And contracts frequently contain ambiguous language.

The causes of interpretation disputes. Why do parties sometimes disagree about what their contracts require? Asked another way, when parties execute formal, written agreements, why don’t they include every term that might be necessary to their deal—and put those terms in clear language—so that there is never a dispute? There are multiple reasons why parties do not—and often cannot—draft contracts that cover every possible topic using unambiguous language.

First, language is a system of symbols by which we express ideas. But, as we all know, language as a medium for expression of ideas is far from perfect. The words a person uses may not fully express the ideas that the person p. 389was trying to convey. Even when people appear to be using the same words, they may attribute different meaning to those words. One reason that the same word, phrase, sentence, paragraph, or entire document can mean different things to different people is that no two persons have exactly the same background. As a result, the context in which people interpret words will always vary for each interpreter. See Restatement (Second) Contracts § 201, Comment b.

Second, parties lack the knowledge and foresight necessary to anticipate every contingency that might be worth addressing in their agreement. Steven J. Burton, Elements of Contract Interpretation § 1.2.2 (2009).

Third, the stakes of most transactions do not justify the costly and lengthy negotiations and drafting that would be necessary to address every issue the parties actually know about. Id.

Fourth, people generally trust each other. Therefore, they assume that (1) nothing will go wrong, and (2) if a problem does arise, that each side will be reasonable, resulting in a fair compromise.

Fifth, many disputes about contractual meaning result from poor contract drafting by the lawyers representing the parties. See E. Allan Farnsworth, Contracts § 7.1 (5th ed. 2004).

Importance of interpretation as a topic. Interpretation is the subject addressed most often by lawyers who work with contracts. Transactional attorneys attempt to identify and eliminate interpretation problems when drafting agreements, in part so that their clients can avoid litigation. And litigators handle disputes over meaning that arise post-formation.

Interpretation versus construction. Some courts and writers have drawn a distinction between interpretation—the process of determining the meaning that the parties attributed to contractual language—and construction, which is the judicial role in determining the legal effect of that language. These materials will ordinarily use the term interpretation to include construction, a usage favored by modern contract theorists because the distinction between interpretation and construction is both cumbersome and usually unnecessary. See E. Allan Farnsworth, Contracts § 7.7 (4th ed. 2004).

Approaches to contract interpretation. There are two general approaches to contract interpretation. The first is called the “textualist approach” or “textualism.” Other names used to describe textualism include the “formalist approach” and the “classical approach.” The second approach is called the “contextualist approach” or “contextualism.” It too has other names, such as the “substantive approach” and the “modern approach.” As you can see based on the alternative names, textualism is associated with classical contract law and contextualism is associated with modern contract law.

Textualist judges follow the “plain meaning rule” or “four corners rule.” Joshua M. Silverstein, Contract Interpretation and the Parol Evidence Rule: Toward Conceptual Clarification, 24 Chapman L. Rev. 89, 96 (2020). That rule sets forth a two-stage process. E. Allan Farnsworth, Contracts § 7.12 (4th ed. 2004). During the first stage, the court assesses whether the disputed p. 390contract language is ambiguous. Id. An ambiguity exists when the relevant contractual language is “reasonably susceptible” to more than one meaning. Pioneer Peat, Inc. v. Quality Grassing & Servs., Inc., 653 N.W.2d 469, 473 (Minn. Ct. App. 2002). The ambiguity determination is a question of law for the judge; in making that determination, a judge following the textualist approach may consider only what is within the “four corners” of the contract itself. See, e.g., Ashwood Cap., Inc. v. OTG Mgmt., Inc., 948 N.Y.S. 2d 292, 297 (App. Div. 2012). If the court concludes that the contract is unambiguous, it simply applies the unambiguous, “plain meaning” of the language to the facts of the case. Steven J. Burton, Elements of Contract Interpretation § 4.2.3 (2009). Extrinsic evidence is never reviewed by the judge (though judges may use dictionaries during the first stage, which are not considered “evidence”). Id. at §§ 2.1.2, 4.2.3. And the case can be disposed of via a motion to dismiss, a motion for summary judgment, or some other preliminary proceeding. Seaco Ins. Co. v. Barbosa, 761 N.E.2d 946, 951 (Mass. 2002); Salewski v. Music, 54 N.Y.S. 3d 203, 205 (App. Div. 2017).

If the judge concludes that the contract is ambiguous, then interpreting the agreement moves to the second stage—resolving the ambiguity. Joshua M. Silverstein, Contract Interpretation and the Parol Evidence Rule: Toward Conceptual Clarification, 24 Chapman L. Rev. 89, 99 (2020). At that stage, interpretation is a question of fact, Archer v. DDK Holdings LLC, 463 S.W.3d 597, 606 (Tex. App. 2015), and extrinsic evidence regarding the contract’s meaning may be considered, Steven J. Burton, Elements of Contract Interpretation § 4.2.3 (2009). All evidence other than the words of the contract and dictionaries is considered “extrinsic evidence”—literally evidence that is external (i.e., extrinsic) to the contract. Examples of such evidence include (a) documents from and testimony regarding the preliminary negotiations, and (b) testimony regarding industry practice.

When the parties do not submit any extrinsic evidence, or when the evidence presented is so one-sided that there is no genuine issue of material fact regarding the contract’s meaning, then the judge resolves the ambiguity, typically via summary judgment. If the parties submit extrinsic evidence and a reasonable jury could rule for either side, then the jury resolves the ambiguity at trial (unless the parties have waived their right to a jury). Nycal Corp. v. Inoco PLC, 988 F. Supp. 296, 299-300 (S.D.N.Y. 1997); Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 212(2) & Comment e. At the second stage, the judge and jury make their respective determinations about what the contract means by looking at all of the evidence (including the language of the agreement) from the perspective of the parties at the moment the contract was formed. Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 202, Comment b.

Because textualist courts conduct the initial ambiguity determination without considering materials beyond the four corners of the document, the text of the contract is often the only evidence reviewed in ascertaining the meaning of the agreement. Hence the name of this interpretive school: “textualism.”

