Contextualism generally involves the same two-stage process as textualism. Steven J. Burton, Elements of Contract Interpretation §§ 4.1, 4.2.2 (2009). But the contextualist approach differs in the method used for establishing whether a contract is ambiguous. According to this view, both the language of the agreement and extrinsic evidence are relevant in deciding whether an ambiguity exists. In other words, at stage one, the judge must consider extrinsic evidence offered by the parties, something forbidden by textualism. Id. § 4.2.2.; see, e.g., Winet v. Price, 6 Cal. Rptr. 2d 554, 557 (Ct. App. 1992). If the judge concludes that the agreement is not reasonably susceptible to the meaning offered by one of the parties—i.e., if textual and extrinsic evidence overwhelmingly supports one side—then the judge resolves the case by motion for summary judgment, directed verdict, or a similar procedural device. BNC Mortg., Inc. v. Tax Pros, Inc., 46 P.3d 812, 819-820 (Wash. Ct. App. 2002). If the judge decides that the agreement is “reasonably susceptible” to different meanings offered by the parties—i.e., a reasonable jury could rule for either side given the textual and extrinsic evidence—then a factual issue exists for the jury (or the judge as trier of fact in a nonjury case). Id. Note that while extrinsic evidence plays a larger role under contextualism, the language of the contract remains the most important evidence in determining contractual meaning under this approach. See Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 212, Comment b. The Restatement (Second) of Contracts endorses contextualism. See, e.g., § 202(1) (“Words and other conduct are interpreted in the light of all the circumstances[.]”); § 202, Comment d (“Meaning is inevitably dependent on context.”).

