Both textualist and contextualist courts generally consider the contractual language and all extrinsic evidence to be relevant at stage two once a contract is determined to be ambiguous. Steven J. Burton, Elements of Contract Interpretation §§ 1.2.3, 5.2 (2009); Alan Schwartz & Robert E. Scott, Contract Interpretation Redux, 119 Yale L.J. 926, 963 n.94 (2010). The essence of their disagreement is over what evidence may be considered during stage one in making the ambiguity determination. Aaron D. Goldstein, The Public Meaning Rule: Reconciling Meaning, Intent, and Contract Interpretation, 53 Santa Clara L. Rev. 73, 80 (2013).

In sum, under textualism, before extrinsic evidence of the context may be considered, ambiguity must be apparent on the face of the agreement. Such an ambiguity is typically called “patent,” “intrinsic,” or “facial.” Under contextualism, extrinsic evidence of the context may be used to establish the existence of an ambiguity. Such an ambiguity is typically called “latent” or “extrinsic.” Put simply (too simply as you will see), textualism recognizes only patent ambiguities, whereas contextualism recognizes both patent and latent ambiguities.

Most contracts scholars who have written on contract interpretation believe that textualism is the majority approach. See, e.g., Joseph M. Perillo, Contracts § 3-10, at 136-137 (7th ed. 2014). However, as one commentator explained, there are “innumerable gradations” of textualism and contextualism in the case law. Peter Linzer, 6 Corbin on Contracts § 25.13, at 146 (Joseph M. Perillo ed., rev. ed. 2010). Moreover, the law of contract interpretation is extraordinarily convoluted. “In virtually every jurisdiction, one finds irreconcilable cases, frequent changes in doctrine, confusion, and cries of despair.” Eric A. Posner, The Parol Evidence Rule, The Plain Meaning Rule, and the Principles of Contract Interpretation, 146 U. Penn. L. Rev. 533, 540 (1998).

Section A of this chapter focuses on textualism. Contextualism will receive considerable attention in Section B. We are saving contextualism for the next p. 393section, in part, because it is easier to understand the details of that interpretive approach once you have a basic grasp of the parol evidence rule, which is discussed at the beginning of Section B.

1.The Ambiguity Determination

As noted in the introduction to this section, the first stage of the interpretive process under textualism or contextualism is the ambiguity determination. The following case is an excellent example of how a textualist court addresses whether a contract is patently ambiguous. As you read the decision, try to identify the following aspects of the case: (1) the negotiations leading up to the contract; (2) the language of the contract; (3) why the parties now have a dispute; (4) the parties’ conflicting interpretations of the contract; (5) the decision and reasoning of the trial court; (6) the legal rules articulated by the majority; (7) the arguments for each interpretation discussed by the majority; and (8) the reasoning of both the majority and the dissent.

Prochazka v. Bee-Three Development, LLC

Arkansas Court of Appeals 2015 Ark. App. 384, 466 S.W.3d 448 (2015)

Brandon J. Harrison, Judge

This appeal asks whether the parties’ purchase agreement supports at least two reasonable interpretations on when Bee-Three Development, LLC could terminate its agreement with the Prochazkas and scuttle the sale of a commercial lot. If the agreement is not open to varying interpretations, then the Pope County Circuit Court’s summary judgment in favor of Bee-Three Development, LLC may be affirmed. If, however, the contract was open to different reasonable interpretations on the termination question, then the court erred as a matter of law in granting summary judgment.

I.

Bee-Three Development, LLC entered into a written agreement to purchase a commercial lot from Robert and Donna Prochazka. During the inspection period Bee-Three terminated the agreement and demanded that the Prochazkas return $7,000 in earnest money. The Prochazkas refused. Bee-Three sued for the earnest money; the Prochazkas counterclaimed for breach of contract and asked that the earnest money be forfeited to them as liquidated damages. Bee-Three then moved for summary judgment, and the circuit court granted the motion, concluding that one of the contract’s three termination clauses unambiguously gave Bee-Three an “absolute” right to terminate the contract within the inspection period. Because we are persuaded that the termination clause is ambiguous when the entire contract is considered, we reverse the summary judgment, reinstate the counterclaim, and remand.

This controversy centers on Article 4 of the purchase agreement, which states:

p. 394
Article 4

Inspection of Property

4.1 Inspection Period. During the Inspection Period, Buyer may conduct soil, engineering, environmental, geotechnical, and other tests with regard to the Property, including phase 1 and phase 11 environmental studies; investigate the availability of necessary permits and licenses and the applicable governmental requirements relating to Buyer’s intended use of the Property, will satisfy itself that the Property is or, in a timeframe and at a cost acceptable to Buyer, can be platted as a separate single tax lot, will satisfy itself that the Property has utilities sufficient for Buyer’s intended use, and that the Land and that the Property has the right of access and determine generally the desirability and utility of the Property for Buyer’s planned use of the Property. . . . Upon the expiration of the Inspection Period, the Earnest Money shall be non-refundable and “at-risk” other than as a result of a default by Seller.

4.2 [Intentionally Omitted]

4.3 Right to Terminate. If Buyer determines, in its sole and absolute discretion, that the Property is not suitable for Buyer’s intended use, then Buyer may terminate this Agreement by notifying Seller in writing of its election to terminate on or before the last day of the Inspection Period. If Buyer timely gives the notice on or before the expiration of the Inspection Period, this Agreement will terminate and the parties will have no further rights, liabilities, or obligations, except for those which are expressly to survive, and the Earnest Money, less the independent consideration paid to Seller shall be returned to Buyer[.]

If Buyer does not timely give written notice of its election to terminate this Agreement, Buyer has no further right to terminate this Agreement pursuant to this Section. Upon the expiration of the Inspection Period the Title Company is irrevocably authorized and directed to deliver the Earnest Money to Seller.

And here is the heart of the circuit court’s ruling on ambiguity as it granted summary judgment to Bee-Three:

When the Parties’ contract reserved to [Bee-Three] the right to terminate the contract “in its sole and absolute discretion,” that right was, by the very words that created it, absolute. To posit an interdependence between the “right to terminate” paragraph of the contract and the “right to inspect” paragraph, when the contract does not support such a nexus, is a violation of the clear meaning of the language and introduces an unwarranted and unjustifiable occasion to impute [an] ambiguity when there really is none.

In line with its finding that there was no ambiguity in the contract, the court ruled that Bee-Three properly exercised a right to terminate the contract, granted its motion for summary judgment, and awarded Bee-Three the $7,000 earnest money and costs. The Prochazkas appeal that decision.

II.

A circuit court may grant a summary judgment when there are no genuine issues of material fact to be decided by a trier of fact and the moving party is entitled to judgment as a matter of law. Benton Cnty. v. Overland Dev. Co., 371 Ark. 559, 268 S.W.3d 885 (2007). After a moving party has established a prima facie entitlement to summary judgment, the opposing party must meet proof with p. 395proof and demonstrate the existence of a material issue of fact. Id. On appeal, we determine if summary judgment was appropriate based on whether the evidentiary items presented by the moving party in support of its motion leave a material fact unanswered. Id. This court views the evidence in the light most favorable to the party against whom the motion was filed, resolving all doubts and inferences against the moving party. Id. Our review is not limited to the pleadings; we usually also focus on the affidavits, if any, and other documents filed by the parties, though this case is a bit different for reasons that will be explained in due course.

Circuit courts initially decide whether a contract is ambiguous. Keller v. Safeco Ins. Co. of Am., 317 Ark. 308, 312, 877 S.W.2d 90, 93 (1994). A contractual provision is ambiguous when, as we stated at the outset, there is doubt or uncertainty as to its meaning so that it is open to at least two reasonable interpretations. Elam v. First Unum Life Ins. Co., 346 Ark. 291, 297, 57 S.W.3d 165, 169 (2001). The primary contract-interpretation rule is to give the parties’ words the meaning that they intended them to have. E.g., Singletary v. Singletary, 2013 Ark. 506, at 10, 431 S.W.3d 234, 240-41. And we must give the words their plain and ordinary meaning. Id. “The best construction is that which is made by viewing the subject of the contract, as the mass of mankind would view it, as it may be safely assumed that such was the aspect in which the parties themselves viewed it.” Id. Another settled rule is that the parties’ intention must be gathered, not from particular words and phrases, but from the entire agreement. Id.

When a contract is unambiguous the circuit court applies the plain language of the parties’ terms and determines as a matter of law how to apply the contract. Roberts Contracting Co. v. Valentine-Wooten Rd. Pub. Facility Bd., 2009 Ark. App. 437, 320 S.W.3d 1; see also Fryer v. Boyett, 64 Ark. App. 7, 978 S.W.2d 304 (1998).

If an ambiguity is present, the circuit court may nonetheless apply the contract as a matter of law if the ambiguity can be resolved by referring to the contract. See Zulpo v. Farm Bureau Mut. Ins. Co., 98 Ark. App. 320, 255 S.W.3d 494 (2007). If the contract’s terms cannot sweep away the initial uncertainty, then the court may look outside the contract. Singletary, supra. Considerable weight may be given to how the parties themselves understood a contract’s terms, as shown by their subsequent statements, acts, and conduct. Id.

III.

The Prochazkas maintain here, as they did in circuit court, that the termination language contained in Section 4.3 is ambiguous because it reasonably has more than one possible meaning. It could be read, as the circuit court did, to allow Bee-Three to terminate the contract within the inspection period because a potential (but undisclosed) tenant had backed out for reasons totally unrelated to the commercial lot’s ability to serve the undisclosed end user’s purposes. Or the contract could be read to mean what the Prochazkas believe it does: the termination must be related to an intended purpose of the inspection period as specified in paragraph 4.1—for example, permits, engineering tests, utilities, rights of access to and from the property, tax-related issues, and the like. Given the reasonable differences in opinion on the parties’ contractual right to terminate, the Prochazkas argue that the court erred “when it substituted its p. 396opinion as the meaning of the language” instead of leaving it “to the trier-of-fact to determine the intent of the parties.”

We begin by asking if the disputed contractual term in Section 4.3 is ambiguous, based on a four-corners reading of the document. See State Auto Prop. & Cas. Ins. Co. v. Ark. Dep’t of Envt’l Quality, 370 Ark. 251, 258, 258 S.W.3d 736, 742 (2007). In doing so we will not at this point consider the affiants’ testimony as is usually done in a summary-judgment case; that extrinsic evidence may not be used to create an ambiguity within the contract where one may not exist. Here again is the main provision at issue: “If Buyer determines, in its sole and absolute discretion, that the Property is not suitable for Buyer’s intended use, then Buyer may terminate this Agreement by notifying Seller in writing of its election to terminate on or before the last day of the Inspection Period.” Two additional termination clauses are present in the contract, and they each appear at different places. The second termination clause appears in Section 4.6 (titled “Utilities”) and allows the contract to be terminated “[i]f Buyer shall determine that any of the above is not true.” The third termination clause appears in Section 5.2 (titled “Covenants”) and allows Bee-Three to have a choice of remedies, including termination, if the Prochazkas made “untrue or misleading” representations or warranties. A reasonable view of the contract, which is opposite Bee-Three’s and the circuit court’s, is that the termination clauses are anchored more or less to the subject matter of the sections in which they appear. If that is so, then the termination clause in Section 4.3 does not empower Bee-Three, as a matter of law, to terminate for the reason it has provided.

A material ambiguity exists because Bee-Three’s right to terminate the purchase agreement could be viewed as being as “absolute” as the circuit court concluded it to be. Our dissenting colleagues see it this way. But in our view, the disputed termination provision can be read to mean that Bee-Three’s exercise of its termination right under Paragraph 4.3 must be substantially related to the purpose of the inspection period and the events and concerns expressed in Article 4. Why else place a termination clause under Article 4? Why not make the argued for “absolute right to terminate” provision a standalone provision, unmoored from any other subject matter or context like an inspection period? See, e.g., Harris Corp. v. Giesting & Assoc., Inc., 297 F.3d 1270, 1272 (11th Cir. 2002) (convenience-term case) (finding contractual language that a party “may terminate this Agreement for convenience at any time upon sixty days written notice to the other” to be unambiguous and allowed the party to terminate the contract for any reason). Moreover, the contract in this case does not define the term “intended use.”

Having decided that there is an ambiguity within the contract that cannot be resolved by the contract itself, we may turn to the affidavits. They show that a genuine issue of material fact exists about the intended operation of Section 4.3’s termination clause. Bee-Three submitted an affidavit from Rob Holliday, who was one of Bee-Three’s managing members. According to him, after Bee-Three had a property under contract it would ensure the property was “viable” for the “specifications of the end user.” The “end use” in this case, Holliday p. 397explained, was that Bee-Three would improve the property and then lease it to a client (the end user). Bee-Three terminated the agreement because, according to Holliday, “the client changed their mind and renewed [a lease] in their current location . . . without a tenant or user we have no need to purchase the land and develop the property.” Holliday’s affidavit does not connect the failure to procure a tenant to some physical, engineering, permitting, or tax-related failure that was discovered during the inspection period. Robert Prochazka filed an opposing affidavit and stated that there were no “discussions about the contract being contingent upon [Bee-Three] securing a tenant.” He testified that Bee-Three shrouded the true purpose of the transaction—including the existence of its anticipated end-user client—in secrecy and should not be allowed to terminate the agreement under the circumstances.

IV.

How the parties intended the ambiguous purchase agreement to operate should be resolved by a trier of fact. See Tri-Eagle Enterprises v. Regions Bank, 2010 Ark. App. 64, at 5-6, 373 S.W.3d 399, 403-04. We therefore reverse the summary judgment, reinstate the Prochazkas’ counterclaim, and remand for further proceedings consistent with this opinion.

Reversed and remanded.

Abramson, Kinard, and Whiteaker, JJ., agree.

Virden, J., concurs.

Gruber, Vaught, Hoofman, and Brown, JJ., dissent.

Bart F. Virden, Judge, concurring.

I concur with the result reached by the majority in this summary-judgment case. While I see no ambiguity in the language regarding termination of the contract[,] . . . I agree that the case should be remanded for trial because I believe there is a genuine issue of material fact as to the buyer’s “intended use” and whether the sellers’ property was suitable for such use.

Rita W. Gruber, Judge, dissenting.

I would affirm the trial court’s order granting summary judgment to Bee-Three and dismissing the Prochazkas’ counterclaim. I would hold, as the trial court did, that there was no unanswered question of material fact regarding Bee-Three’s contractual right to terminate and to receive a refund of its earnest money.

The first rule of interpreting a contract is to give the language employed the meaning that the parties intended, and the court must consider the sense and meanings of the words used by the parties as they are taken and understood in their plain, ordinary meaning. Cranfill v. Union Planters Bank, N.A., 86 Ark. App. 1, 158 S.W.3d 703 (2004). It is the duty of the court to construe a contract according to its unambiguous language without enlarging or extending its terms. Id. (citing North v. Philliber, 269 Ark. 403, 602 S.W.2d 643 (1980)).

In its oral remarks, the trial court found that Bee-Three “had absolute discretion” to terminate and “just clearly exercised that right.” In its written order, the court found the meaning of the contract to be clear:

p. 398
When the Parties’ contract reserved to the Plaintiff/Buyer the right to terminate the contract “in its sole and absolute discretion,” that right was, by the very words that created it, absolute. To posit an interdependence between the “right to terminate” paragraph of the contract and the “right to inspect” paragraph, when the contract does not support such a nexus, is a violation of the clear meaning of the language and introduces an unwarranted and unjustifiable occasion to impute [an] ambiguity where there really is none.

I would hold, just as the trial court did, that there is no foundation to an argument of ambiguity in the written language concerning the right to terminate. To find such an ambiguity is to enlarge and extend the terms of the contract.

Quite simply, the language of the contract is clear. Paragraph 4.1 allows the buyer, during the inspection period, to “determine generally the desirability and utility of the Property for Buyer’s planned use of the Property.” Paragraph 4.3 specifies that the buyer’s determination of unsuitability for intended use, in the buyer’s “sole and absolute discretion,” gives the buyer the right to terminate and to have its earnest money returned if written notice is given before expiration of the inspection period.

Paragraph 4.1, although specifically allowing Bee-Three to inspect the property for environmental, taxation, and zoning issues, still allowed Bee-Three to “determine generally” the desirability of the property for the use it intended. Paragraph 4.3 afforded Bee-Three “sole and absolute” discretion to make the determination that the property lacked suitability for Bee-Three’s intended use—in this case, leasing to an anchor tenant. Bee-Three was not required to convey this intended use to the Prochazkas as a condition of buying. Bee-Three determined the property to be unsuitable, as was allowed by both paragraphs, and exercised its absolute right to terminate.

I dissent from the majority opinion, which expands, in my view, the terms of the contract. The contract’s clear language afforded Bee-Three absolute discretion to determine that the property was not suitable for its use, and this use was not a term of the contract. I am authorized to say that Judges Vaught, Hoofman, and Brown join in this dissenting opinion.

Vaught, Hoofman, and Brown, JJ., join in this dissent.

Notes and Questions
1.Analyzing Prochazka. The judges assigned to Prochazka disagreed over whether the contract was ambiguous. The trial court and the four dissenting appellate judges concluded that the contract unambiguously meant what Bee-Three contended. The concurring judge agreed with this group, but joined the majority on other grounds. And the four appellate judges who exclusively joined the majority opinion thought that the contract was ambiguous. Given all of the arguments addressed in the various opinions and any additional arguments you can come up with, does the contract in Prochazka unambiguously mean what either side claims? Or is the agreement ambiguous as the p. 399majority concluded? And if the contract is ambiguous, what is the most persuasive interpretation of the agreement? In other words, if you were a member of the jury, how would you resolve the ambiguity?

The Prochazka court says it “usually” focuses on affidavits and other documents submitted by the parties at the summary judgment stage, but that “this case is a bit different.” Why is this case different? In other words, why did the court not consider affidavits and documentary evidence during summary judgment in this lawsuit when addressing whether the contract was ambiguous?

2.Subsequent proceedings in Prochazka. After the case was remanded, the Prochazkas and Bee-Three settled the case with the parties splitting the $7,000 in earnest money evenly. Why would the Prochazkas and Bee-Three expend so much time and resources litigating over what many would deem a modest sum like $7,000? The attorneys’ fees for each party could have exceeded $7,000 by a considerable amount.

3.Types of patent ambiguity. “An ambiguity does not arise simply because the parties advance conflicting interpretations of the contract.” Columbia Gas Transmission Corp. v. New Ulm Gas, Ltd., 940 S.W.2d 587, 589 (Tex. 1996). If that were all it took, every agreement that is subject to an interpretive dispute would be “ambiguous.” Instead, an agreement is ambiguous under textualism only when the language of the contract is in fact patently ambiguous.

There are two basic types of patent ambiguities—ambiguities that appear on the face of a contract. The first type is called “vagueness.” Vagueness exists when the ambiguity concerns a question of degree. Farnsworth, § 7.8, at 441-442. For example, suppose a contract says that performance must be completed “during the day.” We know that performance at noon would constitute compliance with the contract. And we know that performing at midnight would constitute a breach. But what about at dusk and dawn? Alternatively, suppose a seller is to provide “blue” items under a contract. At what point does blue shade into another color, such as green or purple?

The second type of ambiguity is best thought of as “either/or ambiguities” or “either/or problems.” Such problems can arise with (1) individual words or short phrases, (2) sentences, and (3) entire contracts. E. Allan Farnsworth, Contracts § 7.8 (5th ed. 2004). Either/or ambiguities with individual words or short phrases are often called “term ambiguities.” Steven J. Burton, Elements of Contract Interpretation § 1.2.2 (2009). Term ambiguity is familiar to most people because individual words frequently have more than one meaning in standard English. To illustrate, the term “bank” can refer to a financial institution or to an area next to a river. Either/or ambiguities that apply to entire contracts are known as “structural ambiguities.” Id. The classic example is where two provisions or sections of a contract conflict with each other.

What type of ambiguity arguably existed in the Prochazka case? Vagueness, either/or, or both?

Note also that while it is helpful conceptually to understand the difference between vagueness and either/or ambiguities so that you know what to look for when reading contracts, courts seldom use the distinction. Courts generally refer to both categories as simply “ambiguities.”

p. 400
4.Ambiguity in the contested respect. A contract is not patently ambiguous merely because it is reasonably susceptible to more than one meaning. Instead, a contract is patently ambiguous only if it is reasonably susceptible to both of the meanings argued for by the litigating parties. Agrigenetics, Inc. v. Pioneer Hi-Bred Int’l, Inc., 758 F. Supp. 2d 766, 771 (S.D. Ind. 2010) (“Ambiguity exists only when both parties’ interpretive positions [are] reasonable.”) (internal quotation marks omitted). This is sometimes referred to as ambiguity “in the contested respect.” Steven J. Burton, Elements of Contract Interpretation 105-106 (2009).

Note that “[e]ven if both parties assert that a contract is unambiguous, a court may [still] hold that [the] contract is ambiguous.” Horseshoe Bay Resort, Ltd. v. CRVI CDP Portfolio, LLC, 415 S.W.3d 370, 377 (Tex. App. 2013). In lawsuits involving contract interpretation, the parties frequently both contend that the contract is unambiguous. The plaintiff argues that the contract unambiguously means one thing and the defendant claims that the contract unambiguously means something else. In such cases, the court can reach one of four rulings at stage one (the ambiguity determination): (1) the contract unambiguously means what the plaintiff claims; (2) the contract unambiguously means what the defendant claims; (3) the contract unambiguously means something in between what the two parties claim; or (4) the contract is ambiguous and the matter must proceed to stage two (resolving the ambiguity).

To illustrate, in Gastar Exploration, Inc. v. Rine, 806 S.E.2d 448 (W. Va. 2017), the grantee of a piece of land argued that the deed unambiguously transferred all of the grantors’ oil and gas rights and the grantors argued that the deed unambiguously transferred none of the grantors’ oil and gas rights. The West Virginia Supreme Court concluded that both of those interpretations were plausible, but also found that the deed may have transferred half of the grantors’ oil and gas rights. Given the existence of three plausible understandings, the court ruled the contract ambiguous, Id. at 457, which is option (4) in the prior paragraph. But had the language of the deed been different, the court could have found that the contract unambiguously meant (1) what the grantee claimed (all rights transferred), (2) what the grantors claimed (no rights transferred), or (3) the middle ground (half the rights transferred).

5.The text of the contract as interpretive evidence. Under both textualism and contextualism, the text of the contract—the actual words written down—is the most important evidence of contractual meaning. Contracts contain different types of text. Most obviously, written contracts always contain what are called “operative clauses” or “operative provisions.” These are the words, phrases, sentences, and paragraphs that specify the parties’ obligations under the agreement.

Contracts also sometimes contain “titles” or “headings.” These are used to classify or organize the operative clauses. And a title can also apply to an entire contract (e.g., “Sales Agreement,” “Arbitration Agreement,” or “Insurance Policy”).

Many contracts contain “recitals.” A recital is a preliminary statement explaining the reasons for entering the agreement and/or showing the p. 401existence of particular facts that constitute the background for the transaction. Normally, each recital begins with the word “whereas.” AmeriPath, Inc. v. Hebert, 447 S.W.3d 319, 331 (Tex. App. 2014); see also Blind Hunting Club, LLC v. Martini, 169 N.E.3d 1121, 1125 (Ind. Ct. App. 2021) (distinguishing “operative provisions” from “recitals”). In drafting contracts lawyers often try to express the contractual intent of their clients in the “whereas” clauses.

Finally, note that contracts can consist of multiple, related documents. For example, a written agreement may contain exhibits or appendices. Alternatively, a contract might expressly incorporate other writings by reference, making those other writings part of the contract. We saw this in Chapter 2 in Ray v. William G. Eurice Bros., Inc., 93 A.2d 272 (Md. 1952), where the contract incorporated various architectural plans and specifications. The “text of the contract” includes the language in all writings that constitute the parties’ agreement.

6.Dictionaries as interpretive tools. Courts may consult dictionaries when interpreting contracts, statutes, and constitutions. And they do so frequently. Dictionaries generally do not constitute “evidence” in the technical sense of being governed by the rules of evidence you will study in your Evidence course. And courts usually do not consider dictionaries to be extrinsic evidence. See, e.g., Fireman’s Fund Ins. Co. v. Great Am. Ins. Co., 10 F. Supp. 3d 460, 494 (S.D.N.Y. 2014); Anchor Savs. Bank, FSB v. United States, 121 Fed. Cl. 296, 311 (2015).

7.Criticism of the textualist theory of language. Some scholars and judges have rejected textualism because they dispute the claim, purportedly made by textualists, that words are capable of having only one precise meaning, which is supposedly necessary for language to be unambiguous. As Holmes said, “it is not true that in practice (and I know no reason why theory should disagree with the facts) a given word or even a given collocation of words has one meaning and no other. A word generally has several meanings, even in a dictionary.” Oliver Wendell Holmes, The Theory of Legal Interpretation, 12 Harv. L. Rev. 417 (1899); see also Margaret N. Kniffin, 5 Corbin on Contracts § 24.7 (Joseph M. Perillo ed., rev. ed. 1998) (arguing that meaning can never be plain without considering extrinsic evidence of “at least some of the surrounding circumstances”).

Is this a persuasive critique of textualism? In particular, does the criticism fairly characterize what textualist courts mean when they declare that a contract is “unambiguous”? In analyzing this question, consider the material in Note 4 above regarding ambiguity in the contested respect.

2.The Resolution of Ambiguity

Resolving ambiguities and the standard of interpretation. Assume a court has ruled that a contract is patently ambiguous. The next step is to resolve the ambiguity. That requires the court to apply what is known as a “standard of interpretation.” “A standard of interpretation is the test applied by the law to words and to other manifestations of intention in order to determine the meaning to be p. 402given to them.” Restatement (First) of Contracts § 227; accord Richard Lord, 11 Williston on Contracts § 31:1 (4th ed. 2012). There are two basic types of interpretation standards—subjective standards and objective standards.

Objective standards focus on what a reasonable person would understand the contract to mean given the text and some or all of the relevant extrinsic evidence. Behrens v. S.P. Constr. Co., 904 A.2d 676, 681 (N.H. 2006) (“An objective standard places a reasonable person in the position of the parties, and interprets a disputed term according to what a reasonable person would expect it to mean under the circumstances.”). Subjective standards focus on the actual intent of the parties. Under subjectivism, the contract means what one or both of the parties actually understood it to mean when they formed the contract. See Restatement (First) of Contracts § 227, Comment a; Richard Lord, 11 Williston on Contracts § 31:1 (4th ed. 2012).

The difference between objective and subjective standards can be thought of in terms of on whom the standard focuses. Under objective standards, the test is what would a reasonable person think, not what the parties actually thought. With subjective standards, by contrast, the focus is on the actual understanding of the parties, not what a reasonable person would think. Note further that both objective and subjective standards are applied as of the time the contract was entered into. In other words, what matters is what a reasonable person would have thought (objective) or what the parties actually thought (subjective) at the time the contract was entered into. Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 202, Comment b; Taylor v. NationsBank, N.A., 776 A.2d 645, 653 (Md. 2001).

The leading objective standard. The objective standard used most often at stage two of the interpretive process when resolving the ambiguity is this: What would a reasonable person understand the contract to mean if that person was familiar with all of the surrounding circumstances, including the contract, standard English, industry practice, the parties’ preliminary negotiations, and other aspects of the context. See Restatement (First) of Contracts § 230. We refer to this as “the” objective standard throughout the rest of this chapter. Professor Williston, both in his treatise and in the original Restatement, defended the objective standard even if application of the standard led to an interpretation that neither of the parties intended. See Restatement § 230, Comment b. Consider Illustration 1 to § 230 of the first Restatement:

1. In an integrated agreement A promises to sell, and B promises to buy certain patents. A intends to sell only English patents on a certain invention. B understands that A promises to sell the English, French, and American patents on the invention. If a reasonably intelligent person . . . would understand the agreement to state a promise to sell the English and American patents, but not the French patents, there is a contract and A and B are bound by that meaning.

The leading subjective standard—the Restatement (Second) of Contracts. In his treatise, Professor Corbin laid the foundation for the leading subjective standard, which is now set forth in the Restatement (Second) and is discussed below. According to Corbin, in interpreting a contract, a court should answer two questions: (1) Whose meaning controls the interpretation of the contract? and p. 403(2) What was that party’s meaning? 3 Corbin on Contracts § 536 (1960), at 31-32. Unlike Professor Williston, Corbin thought that it would be absurd for a court to give a contract a meaning that neither of the parties intended.

If a court says that parties are bound by this meaning even though neither one of them held it, it is very probable that the court believes, either that both of them did in fact hold it, or that one of them did and the other had reason to know that he did. But to hold that, although A intends to sell Blackacre and B intends to buy Whiteacre, A must convey and B must accept Greenacre because their “integration” would so be understood by C or by a large community of third persons, is to hold justice up to ridicule.

3 Corbin on Contracts § 539 (1960), at 81. The updated version of the Corbin treatise continues to embrace the Restatement (Second)’s subjective approach which “would never enable a court to impose an interpretation on the parties that was reasonably held by a third party but that differed from what the contracting parties understood or had reason to understand.” Margaret N. Kniffin, 5 Corbin on Contracts § 24.6 (Joseph M. Perillo ed., 1998).

The Restatement standard is set forth in Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 201. This standard has multiple steps. And note that at each step, the court considers the same materials as it would under the objective standard—the language of the agreement, dictionaries, and all (or virtually all) extrinsic evidence. (We use “step” here to distinguish the pieces of the Restatement (Second) standard from the “stages” of textualism and contextualism.)

Step 1 is set forth in § 201(1). Under § 201(1), if both parties attach the same meaning to contractual language, that meaning governs. The parties’ mutual understanding controls even if it is different from the interpretation of a reasonable person. But if the parties do not share the same meaning, then you must continue to step 2, which is set forth in § 201(2).

Section 201(2) has the interpreter ask two questions. What did each party actually know about what the other party thought the contract meant? See § 201(2)(a). And what should each party have known about what the other party thought the contract meant (often called “constructive knowledge”)? See § 201(2)(b). And, of course, there is a third possibility—a party may have had no actual or constructive knowledge about what the other party thought the contract meant. Thus, there are three levels of a knowledge a party can have—actual knowledge, constructive knowledge, and no knowledge.

Keep in mind that the type of knowledge that is relevant under § 201(2) is what the parties knew or should have known about what the other side thought the contract meant. This means that § 201(2) is concerned with the following: What did the first party know about what the second party thought the contract meant? And what did the second party know about what the first party thought the contract meant? See generally § 201, Comment b.

Section 201 sets forth two rules. First, § 201(2)(a) provides that if one party actually knows what the other party thought the contract meant, but the other party only had either constructive knowledge or no knowledge of what the first party thought the contract meant, then the second party’s interpretation p. 404governs. Second, § 201(2)(b) provides that if one party should have known what the other party thought the contract meant, but the other party had no knowledge of what the first party thought the contract meant, then the second party’s interpretation governs. Some commentators have described § 201(2) as creating a “fault” standard: whichever party is less at fault—whichever party had less knowledge about what the other party thought the contract meant—wins the interpretive dispute. See Steven J. Burton, Elements of Contract Interpretation § 2.5 (2009); E. Allan Farnsworth, Contracts § 7.9 (5th ed. 2004).

Suppose that at step 2 the court concludes that the parties had the same level of knowledge about each others’ understanding of the contract—they both had actual knowledge, both had constructive knowledge, or both had no knowledge about what the other side thought the contract meant. In that case, the Restatement provides a third step: neither party is bound by the meaning of the other party, or, as it is sometimes said, there was “no meeting of the minds.” See Restatement § 201(3). How does the court proceed in that situation?

The Restatement (Second) of Contracts’ subjective standard—when neither party’s meaning governs. When neither party is bound by the other party’s meaning, the courts generally treat the contract as if the disputed provision is an indefinite term, like those we studied in Chapter 2, Section A.4. Put more specifically, the court will treat the ambiguous term as if it is really a gap in the contract. There are three possible results here.

First, if the gap concerns a term that is not material, the courts will fill the gap by adding a term to the contract that is reasonable in the circumstances. See Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 201, Comment d; id. § 204. Second, if the gap concerns a term that is material and the parties have either (a) not performed yet, or (b) performed so little that the performance can be undone, then the contract is nullified. Remember, under the rules governing indefinite terms, if there is a problem with a material term in an agreement, then the parties actually have no contract. See Restatement (Second) § 212, Illustration 1: “In [a written] agreement A promises to sell and B to buy described real estate. A intends to sell Blackacre; B intends to buy Whiteacre. The writing reasonably describes Greenacre, and neither party has any more reason than the other to know of the misdescription. There is no contract.”

Third, if the gap concerns a term that is material and the parties have performed to the point where it is too late to undo the performance and conclude that no contract exists, then once again the court will fill the gap by adding a term that is reasonable in the circumstances. See Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 204.

Professor Prince has addressed this topic and noted that gaps in the contract may result from lack of foresight, incomplete bargaining, or excessive initial optimism about performance of the contract. Harry G. Prince, Contract Interpretation in California: Plain Meaning, Parol Evidence and Use of the “Just Result” Principle, 31 Loy. L.A. L. Rev. 557, 619-620 (1998). Professor p. 405Prince argues that in such cases, courts should not strain to apply principles of interpretation to fill the gap, but should instead strive to achieve a just outcome:

A tremendously better approach would be to simply recognize that, in such cases of lacunae within the contract terms, the courts are empowered to weigh a number of equitable factors and attempt to reach a just result. Those equitable factors would include addressing public interests and public good, allowing for sharing of unanticipated losses and gains, avoiding the award of a windfall to one party at the cost of denying recovery to the other side, and considering the parties’ good or bad faith behavior.

Id. at 650.

The rules governing how courts fill gaps by adding terms to a contract is a subject we will consider in more detail in Chapter 6.

The Frigaliment case. The following case focuses on the resolution of a contractual ambiguity. As you read the decision, try to answer the following questions: How should the ambiguity in Frigaliment be resolved applying the objective standard of interpretation? How should the ambiguity be resolved applying the Restatement (Second) standard of interpretation? Which piece or pieces of evidence do you think most strongly support your conclusions? Which approach to resolving ambiguity did the Frigaliment court actually employ?
